    "message": {
     "content": "### The Bottom Line\n\n**Do not accept this offer as-is.** \n\nWhile **$1.08M equity over 4 years** is a respectable baseline for a Google Senior Product Manager (L6), for someone who is already an established IC6 at Meta making **$526k/yr**, this offer is essentially **lateral**. \n\nTaking a lateral package when you are happy where you are, leaving behind an established track record, and taking on switching risk does not make financial sense. However, because you were sourced passively and have strong standing at Meta, you have **maximum leverage** to negotiate a top-of-band package.\n\n---\n\n### Offer Breakdown vs. Current Meta Comp\n\n| Component | Current (Meta IC6) | Google L6 Offer | Difference / Observation |\n| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |\n| **Base Salary** | $240,000 | $235,000 | **-$5,000** (Below your current base; range max is $271k) |\n| **Bonus Target** | ~$36,000 (15%) | $47,000 (20%) | **+$11,000** |\n| **Annual Equity (Avg)** | ~$250,000 | $270,000 | **+$20,000** ($1.08M / 4 yrs) |\n| **Sign-On Bonus** | N/A | $0 | **$0** (No transition buffer) |\n| **Year 1 Target TC** | **~$526,000** | **~$552,000** | **+~$26,000 (~5% increase)** |\n\n#### Key Takeaways:\n1. **Base Salary Step-Down:** Google is offering you $5,000 *less* in base pay than you currently make, despite their posted range going up to $271,000.\n2. **Missing Sign-On Bonus:** At L6, Google frequently awards sign-on bonuses ($50,000\u2013$120,000) to offset unvested equity left behind at competing tech companies.\n3. **The Recruiter\u2019s \"Refresh Stacking\" Pitch:** Google refreshes do stack, but Meta also issues annual refresh grants. You should never discount an initial offer based on non-guaranteed future performance grants.\n4. **Vesting Schedule Nuance:** Clarify the exact vesting schedule. Google often uses front-loaded vesting (e.g., 33%/33%/22%/12% or 38%/32%/20%/10%) rather than a flat 25% per year. If it is front-loaded, Year 1 cash is higher, but Years 3 and 4 drop significantly unless performance refreshes are aggressive.\n\n---\n\n### What to Ask For (Your Counteroffer)\n\nTarget top-of-band L6 compensation, aiming for **$620,000 \u2013 $660,000+ Year 1 Total Comp**.\n\n#### 1. Base Salary: Ask for $255,000 \u2013 $265,000\n* **Rationale:** You should not take a base pay reduction coming from Meta. The posted band max is $271,000, so $260,000 sits comfortably within the upper quartile.\n\n#### 2. Equity: Ask for $1,300,000 \u2013 $1,400,000 over 4 years\n* **Rationale:** This brings the annualized equity portion to ~$325,000\u2013$350,000/yr. At Google L6, equity is the easiest lever for the compensation committee to expand.\n\n#### 3. Sign-On Bonus: Ask for $75,000 \u2013 $100,000\n* **Rationale:** You have ~$200k in unvested Meta stock vesting in the next 12 months, plus an upcoming bonus cycle. A sign-on bonus is a standard tool Google uses to bridge this transition cost.\n\n---\n\n### How to Execute the Negotiation\n\nCall your recruiter and deliver a collaborative but firm counter. Frame it around enthusiasm for the role paired with the economic reality of leaving Meta.\n\n#### Sample Script / Talking Points:\n\n> *\"Thank you again for the offer. I really enjoyed meeting the team, and I\u2019m genuinely excited about the scope and product direction here at Google.*\n>\n> *As you know, I wasn\u2019t actively looking when you reached out, and I\u2019m in a very strong position at Meta where my total compensation is around $526k, with a higher base salary than what is currently in the offer. Taking into account the equity I would walk away from over the next 12 months, this initial offer is essentially lateral for me.*\n>\n> *I\u2019d love to make this work, but to make the move compelling, I\u2019m looking for:*\n> 1. *A base salary of **$260,000** (to avoid taking a step backward on fixed comp).*\n> 2. *An equity grant of **$1.35M** over 4 years.*\n> 3. *A sign-on bonus of **$75,000** to bridge the unvested stock and bonus I leave behind.*\n>\n> *If we can get to these numbers, I\u2019d be thrilled to sign immediately and decline any other internal Meta opportunities.\"*\n\n---\n\n### Next Steps & Decision Tree\n\n* **If Google meets you near your target ($620k+ Y1):** **Accept.** You get a meaningful compensation jump, a strong reset on your equity timeline, and a fresh trajectory at Google.\n* **If Google makes a moderate revision ($575k\u2013$600k Y1 with sign-on):** Evaluate role scope, product area, and long-term career growth. If you like the team better than Meta, it\u2019s viable.\n* **If Google refuses to negotiate:** **Walk away.** Staying at Meta as an IC6 earning $526k+ with established momentum is strictly better than switching for a flat package.",
